  <content>
    <section>  <title>Question #1: Simplify Expressions Using Properties of Exponents</title>

      <para>Which expression is correct?</para>
    </section>
  <section><title>Simplify Expressions Using Properties of Exponents</title>
<para>In this unit, remember to double click on mathematical expressions/equations to enlarge, if needed.</para>
<para>In the expression \(y^4 \cdot y^2\) :</para>
<para>\(y^4=y \cdot y \cdot y \cdot y\)</para>
<para>The exponent 4 means there are 4 factors of \(y\).</para>
<para>\(y^2=y \cdot y\)</para>
<para>The exponent 2 means there are 2 factors of \(y\).</para>
<para>\(y^4 \cdot y^2=y \cdot y \cdot y \cdot y \cdot y \cdot y\).</para>
<para>There are 6 factors of \(y\), which can be written as \(y^6\).</para>
<para>\(y^4 \cdot y^2=y^6\)</para>
<para>Notice the exponent is the sum of the exponents of the factors.</para>
<para>When you multiply exponential expressions with the same base, you keep the base and add the exponents.</para>
<note>
  <para><emphasis> Product Property for Exponents </emphasis></para>
  
  <para>If \(a\) is a real number and \(m\) and \(n\) are integers, then</para>
  <para>\(a^m \cdot a^n=a^{m+n}\)</para>
  <para>To multiply with like bases, add the exponents.</para>
</note>

<para>In the expression \(\frac {y^5} {y^3}\):</para>
<para>\(y^5=y \cdot y \cdot y \cdot y \cdot y\)</para>
<para>The exponent 5 means there are 5 factors of \(y\).</para>
<para>\(y^3=y \cdot y \cdot y\)</para>
<para>The exponent 3 means there are 3 factors of \(y\).</para>
<para>
  <m:math>
    <m:mfrac>
      <m:msup>
        <m:mi>y</m:mi>
        <m:mn>5</m:mn>
      </m:msup>
      <m:msup>
        <m:mi>y</m:mi>
        <m:mn>3</m:mn>
      </m:msup>
    </m:mfrac>
    <m:mo>=</m:mo>
    <m:mfrac>
      <m:mrow>
        <m:mi>y</m:mi>
        <m:mo>·</m:mo>
        <m:mi>y</m:mi>
        <m:mo>·</m:mo>
        <m:menclose notation="updiagonalstrike">
          <m:mi>y</m:mi>
        </m:menclose>
        <m:mo>·</m:mo>
        <m:menclose notation="updiagonalstrike">
          <m:mi>y</m:mi>
        </m:menclose>
        <m:mo>·</m:mo>
        <m:menclose notation="updiagonalstrike">
          <m:mi>y</m:mi>
        </m:menclose>
      </m:mrow>
      <m:mrow>
        <m:menclose notation="updiagonalstrike">
          <m:mi>y</m:mi>
        </m:menclose>
        <m:mo>·</m:mo>
        <m:menclose notation="updiagonalstrike">
          <m:mi>y</m:mi>
        </m:menclose>
        <m:mo>·</m:mo>
        <m:menclose notation="updiagonalstrike">
          <m:mi>y</m:mi>
        </m:menclose>
      </m:mrow>
    </m:mfrac>
    <m:mo>=</m:mo>
    <m:mi>y</m:mi>
    <m:mo>·</m:mo>
    <m:mi>y</m:mi>
  </m:math>
</para>
<para>There are 2 factors of \(y\), which can be written as \(y^2\).</para>
<para>\(\frac {y^5}{y^3}=y^2\)</para>
<para>Notice the exponent is the difference of the exponents of the factors.</para>
<para>When you divide exponential expressions with the same base, you keep the base and subtract the exponents.</para>

<note>
  <para><emphasis> Quotient Property for Exponents </emphasis></para>
  
  <para>If \(a\) is a real number, \(a\neq0\), and \(m\) and \(n\) are integers, then</para>
  <para>\(\frac{a^m}{a^n}=a^{m-n}\), \(m&gt;n\) and \(\frac{a^m}{a^n}=\frac1{a^{n-m}}\), \(n&gt;m\)</para>
</note>

<para>Here’s how to simplify expressions using properties of exponents.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated">
  <item>\(x^2 \cdot x^3\)</item>
</list>
  <para>The bases are the same. To multiply, use the Product Property for Exponents.</para>
  <para>\(x^2 \cdot x^3=x^{2+3}=x^5\). To multiply with like bases, add the exponents.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated">
  <item>\(y \cdot y^4\)</item>
</list>
  <para>The bases are the same. To multiply, use the Product Property for Exponents.</para>
  <para>Remember: A variable with no exponent shown has an exponent of \(1\), \(y=y^1\).</para>
  <para>\(y \cdot y^4=y^1 \cdot y^4=y^{1+4}=y^5\). To multiply with like bases, add the exponents.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated">
  <item> \(\frac {x^5}{x^2}\)</item>
</list>
  <para>The bases are the same. To divide, use the Quotient Property of Exponents.</para>
  <para>\(\frac {x^5}{x^2}=x^{5-2}=x^3\). To divide with like bases, subtract the exponents.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated">
  <item> \(\frac {x^4yz}{x^2y^2z}\)</item>
</list>
  <para>The expression contains like bases. Use the Quotient Property of Exponents.</para>
  <para>\(\frac{x^4yz}{x^2y^2z}=\frac{x^4y^1z^1}{x^2y^2z^1}=\frac{x^{4-2}z^{1-1}}{y^{2-1}}=\frac{x^2z^0}{y^1}=\frac{x^2}y\). To divide with like bases, subtract the exponents.</para>
  <para>Remember: Any nonzero number to the zero power is 1.</para>

<note class="try">
<title>Try It: Simplify Expressions Using Properties of Exponents</title>
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>Simplify the expressions.</para>

    

    <para>\(x^3 \cdot x^4\)</para>
    <para>Write down your answers. Then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Here is how to simplify expressions using properties of exponents.</para>
  
  <para>\(x^3 \cdot x^4=x^{3+4}=x^7\). To multiply with like bases, add the exponents.</para>

</solution></exercise>
</example>
<!--Q#2-->
<!--Interaction End -->


